# Telemetry Standards

## Before applying this rule
Structured logging (requestId/traceId correlation, queryable metadata fields) is standard practice at organizations with a log aggregator (Datadog, Splunk, CloudWatch) — it is not how most projects log. Check first whether the project already uses a structured logger (`pino`, `winston`, `structlog`, `zap`, or similar — check the manifest file and existing log call shapes). **If no structured logger is configured, do not apply this rule at all** — plain `console.log`/`print` is the norm for that project and flagging it is just noise.

## The rule (only once a structured logger is confirmed present)
Prefer a **structured object** containing the mandatory metadata fields over a plain string log call.

Unstructured logs like `logger.error("something went wrong")` or `console.log(data)` are unqueryable in Datadog, Splunk, CloudWatch, or any structured log aggregator — they make incident debugging significantly harder in a project that has already invested in one.

## Mandatory metadata fields
At minimum, every log call must include:

| Field | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| `requestId` / `traceId` | Correlates all log lines for a single request or trace |
| `level` | Explicit severity — don't rely on the call site method name alone |
| `message` | Human-readable description of the event |

Additional fields by event type:
- Auth/user events: `userId` or `sessionId`
- Data mutations: `entityType`, `entityId`
- External calls: `service`, `method`, `durationMs`
- Errors: `error.message`, `error.stack`, `error.code`

## Correct pattern
```typescript
logger.error({
  requestId: ctx.requestId,
  userId: ctx.userId,
  message: 'Payment authorisation failed',
  error: { message: err.message, code: err.code },
})
```
Same shape in any language: a structured object/dict with `requestId`, `level`, `message`, and context fields — never a bare string.

## What to suggest (structured logger present, request-handling code)

Gently suggest, don't block, when you see:
- `logger.info("string")` / `logger.error("string")` — plain strings passed to an already-configured structured logger, in request-handling or production code paths
- A log call missing `requestId` / `traceId` when nearby calls in the same file already include it (i.e. the project has a convention this call broke)

## What not to flag
- Test files (`*.test.*`, `*.spec.*`, `*_test.*`, `tests/`)
- CLI tools or scripts where structured logging is inappropriate
- Debug-only code clearly marked with a feature flag or env guard
- `console.log` used for local debugging that the developer clearly intends to remove or that isn't in a request/production path
- Any project without an already-configured structured logger (see "Before applying this rule" above) — do not suggest adding one unprompted

## Do not silently refactor
Suggest non-compliant calls and explain what fields are missing. Do not rewrite logging calls without asking — the developer needs to supply the correct context values (requestId, userId, etc.) from their execution context.
